{"kind": "report", "step": 17, "ts": "2026-11-19T15:07:12.896285+00:00", "data": {"category": "dns", "host": "dns1.lan", "component": "dns_rate_limit", "cause": "The primary-dns resolver on dns1.lan rate-limits by /24 subnet at only 20 q/s, so whenever the LAN briefly exceeds that, queries are dropped; during those bursts clients fall back to gw.lan, whose unbound also cannot answer local names (its private-address rebinding filter strips the 10.20.0.50 answer for nas.lan, returning NOERROR with 0 answers), producing intermittent ENOTFOUND for .lan names while internet names still resolve via gw's upstreams.", "evidence": ["journalctl -u primary-dns on dns1.lan: three burst events today (02:14, 09:41, 13:02) 'ratelimit: 10.20.0.0/24 exceeded 20 q/s, dropping N queries', each back under limit within seconds \u2014 matches alerts a few times a day that self-clear in a minute or two.", "/opt/primary-dns/config.yaml: ratelimit: 20 with ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4: 24 \u2014 one bucket for the entire client subnet, and rewrites serve *.lan/nas.lan as 10.20.0.50 from this server.", "dig @10.20.0.3 nas.lan from mon.lan: normal NOERROR answer 10.20.0.50 (TTL 10) \u2014 resolution works when not rate-limited.", "dig @10.20.0.1 nas.lan from mon.lan and drill nas.lan @127.0.0.1 on gw.lan: NOERROR but ANSWER: 0 \u2014 gw's fallback path returns an empty answer for local names.", "/var/unbound/unbound.conf on gw.lan: forwards lan. to 10.20.0.3 yet applies private-address: 10.0.0.0/8 rebinding filtering, so the .lan answers are stripped; internet names forward normally, explaining why only local names fail."], "action": "change_config", "recommendation": "Raise dns1.lan's rate limit (or set ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4 to 32 / exempt the managed LAN) so normal LAN bursts aren't dropped; secondarily, fix gw.lan's unbound so its private-address filter doesn't strip the legitimate .lan zone answers, restoring a working fallback for local names. No reboot or hardware action needed.", "detail": "primary-dns rate limiter drops bursts for the whole 10.20.0.0/24", "contributing": {"host": "gw.lan", "component": "dns_resolver_config"}}}
